{#-
  The export document.

  WHAT THIS FILE IS ALLOWED TO DO: print. Every figure below arrives as an
  already-formatted string on `doc`; there is no arithmetic here, no `sum`, no
  `|float` and no `round`. Section 7.6.1 leaves the front end no calculation but
  unit conversion, and a server-side template that summed a column would be the
  same defect one layer down -- a second calculation site with no golden case
  behind it. `api/pdf_render.py` builds this context out of the engine's own
  `CalculationResult` and does no arithmetic either.

  WHAT IT MUST NOT DO: drop the placeholder warning. See the `mock-warning`
  block below, and `api/pdf_render.py::_assert_mock_warning_present`, which
  refuses to hand this document to WeasyPrint at all if an edit here removes
  it while the factor set is mock.

  NAMES ARE NOT TRANSLATED. `sector`, `food_category`, `destination` and
  `metric` names are staff-typed rows out of the taxonomy (section 2.1). They
  are printed exactly as they were typed, in whatever language they were typed
  in, in every locale this document renders in.

  EVERYTHING ELSE IS. Every label, heading and sentence below arrives on `doc`
  already translated into the document's own language, out of the same
  `web/locales/*.json` catalogues the calculator page reads
  (`api/i18n.py`). There is no English fallback: a catalogue missing one of
  these strings raises rather than printing English into the middle of a
  translated report.

  AND NOTHING HERE MIRRORS ANYTHING. `<html dir>` two lines down is the whole
  of the right-to-left support; Pango runs the Unicode bidirectional algorithm
  from it, and `results.css` uses logical properties throughout so every
  accent bar, indent and column alignment follows without a rule being
  written twice.
-#}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{ doc.lang }}" dir="{{ doc.dir }}">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>{{ doc.title }}</title>
</head>
<body>

{#- THE PLACEHOLDER WARNING.

    A DIRECT CHILD OF <body>, first in the flow, above the title and above
    every figure. Not inside the header, not inside a section, not inside any
    element that a later edit could collapse, clip, float or paginate away --
    there is no ancestor here but <body> itself.

    Its inner flag element is what `results.css` reads with `string-set` to
    repeat "placeholder data" in the running header of every page, so the
    banner is both a block at the top and a mark on each sheet.

    This block is not optional and there is no parameter that suppresses it:
    `doc.is_mock` is `result.is_mock` off the engine's own result, and while
    open item O-1 stands -- the client has supplied no real emissions factors
    -- it is true of every calculation this system can perform. A PDF is the
    artefact most likely to be read months later by someone who was not in the
    room; it has to say so on its face. -#}
{% if doc.is_mock %}
<aside class="mock-warning" role="note">
  <strong class="mock-warning__flag">{{ doc.mock_flag }}</strong>
  <p class="mock-warning__body">{{ doc.mock_body }}</p>
</aside>
{% endif %}

{#- THE TITLE BLOCK (Task 5). What it is (the heading), who produced it, when
    it was generated, and the factor-set version it used - the client's own
    words were that the document "lacks brand character", and this is the
    fix: a masthead rather than a bare heading. `.cover__mark` is the brand's
    half-circle, used singly at large scale per the guideline's own wording;
    `results.css` draws it as a flex sibling of the text so no title in any
    of the twenty-one languages can run under it. Every word here is either
    already a catalogue key the front end asks for elsewhere (§7.6's own
    constraint - see `api/pdf_render.py`'s `_LABELS` comment) or a value with
    no language of its own: the generated stamp is ISO-8601-shaped and the
    factor-set token is `GWP100`, for the same reason neither needs a
    dictionary entry. -#}
<header class="cover">
  {#- THE LOGO ITSELF, not just the half-circle drawn from it. Review finding
      on the first title block: the masthead worked but the Kai Commitment
      logo appeared nowhere, so a green shape and a byline carried the whole
      identity. `kai-commitment-logo.png` is the exact file `web/home.html`,
      `web/methodology.html` and `web/stats.html` already print on a light
      ground (`api/assets/kai-commitment-logo.png` is a byte-identical copy,
      the same discipline `api/assets/locales/` follows for the catalogues) -
      placed at its own natural size, never recoloured, cropped or redrawn,
      because the brand guideline is explicit that the mark may not be
      altered. `alt=""`: decorative here, since `doc.title`, `doc.produced_by`
      and the half-circle beside it already say who this document is from in
      text a screen reader or a text extraction already reads. -#}
  <img class="cover__logo" src="kai-commitment-logo.png" alt="">
  <div class="cover__mark" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="cover__body">
    <h1>{{ doc.title }}</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">{{ doc.subtitle }}</p>
    <p class="cover__meta">{{ doc.produced_by }} &middot; {{ doc.generated_at }} &middot; {{ doc.factor_set }}</p>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="summary">
  <div class="summary__item">
    <span class="summary__label">{{ doc.labels.total_mass }}</span>
    <span class="summary__value">{{ doc.total_kg }} kg</span>
  </div>
  {#- §4.6's totals-level share of production, in its own three states -
      `api/pdf_render.py::_production_share_context` decides which of
      `complete`, `incomplete` and `not_supplied` applies and this only
      prints what it returns. The note explains an `incomplete` or
      `not_supplied` value; a `complete` percentage carries none. -#}
  <div class="summary__item">
    <span class="summary__label">{{ doc.labels.production_share }}</span>
    <span class="summary__value">{{ doc.production_share.value }}</span>
  </div>
  {#- The factor set and the methane horizon in one tile. No catalogue key
      names the horizon, and one English label in a Tamil report is worse than
      a compact one: `GWP100` is the ISO notation and reads the same in every
      language. `api/pdf_render.py` joins the two; nothing is computed here. -#}
  <div class="summary__item">
    <span class="summary__label">{{ doc.labels.factor_set }}</span>
    <span class="summary__value">{{ doc.factor_set }}</span>
  </div>
</div>
{% if doc.production_share.note %}
<p class="summary-note">{{ doc.production_share.note }}</p>
{% endif %}

<h2>{{ doc.labels.totals }}</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>{{ doc.labels.metric }}</th>
      <th class="figure">{{ doc.labels.current }}</th>
      <th class="figure">{{ doc.labels.alternative }}</th>
      <th class="figure">{{ doc.labels.net_benefit }}</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {% for row in doc.totals %}
    <tr>
      <td>{{ row.name }} <span class="unit">({{ row.unit }})</span></td>
      <td class="figure">{{ row.current }}</td>
      <td class="figure">{{ row.alternative }}</td>
      <td class="figure">{{ row.net_benefit }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>

{% if doc.destinations %}
<h2>{{ doc.labels.destinations }}</h2>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>{{ doc.labels.destination }}</th>
      <th class="figure">{{ doc.labels.current }} (kg)</th>
      <th class="figure">{{ doc.labels.alternative }} (kg)</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {% for row in doc.destinations %}
    <tr>
      <td>{{ row.name }}</td>
      <td class="figure">{{ row.current }}</td>
      <td class="figure">{{ row.alternative }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
{% endif %}

{% if doc.money %}
<h2>{{ doc.labels.money }}</h2>
<table>
  <tbody>
    {% for row in doc.money %}
    <tr>
      <td>{{ row.name }}</td>
      {#- §4.6: a field can carry a translated sentence rather than a figure
          (partial coverage - see `api/pdf_render.py::_money_rows`), and that
          sentence must wrap like ordinary text rather than being held to
          `.figure`'s `white-space: nowrap`, which exists for numbers. -#}
      {% if row.is_note %}
      <td class="money-note">{{ row.value }}</td>
      {% else %}
      <td class="figure">{{ row.value }}</td>
      {% endif %}
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
{% endif %}

{% if doc.equivalences %}
<h2>{{ doc.labels.equivalences }}</h2>
{#- The engine interpolated these labels itself (section 3's interpolation
    rule), so the string below is printed verbatim and never re-formatted. -#}
<ul class="equivalences">
  {% for label in doc.equivalences %}
  <li>{{ label }}</li>
  {% endfor %}
</ul>
{% endif %}

{% if doc.entries %}
<h2>{{ doc.labels.entries }}</h2>
{% for entry in doc.entries %}
<section class="entry">
  <h3 class="entry__heading">{{ entry.sector }} &middot; {{ entry.food_category }}</h3>
  <p class="entry__meta">{{ doc.labels.total_mass }}: {{ entry.total_kg }} kg</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>{{ doc.labels.metric }}</th>
        <th class="figure">{{ doc.labels.current }}</th>
        <th class="figure">{{ doc.labels.alternative }}</th>
        <th class="figure">{{ doc.labels.net_benefit }}</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for row in entry.metrics %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ row.name }} <span class="unit">({{ row.unit }})</span></td>
        <td class="figure">{{ row.current }}</td>
        <td class="figure">{{ row.alternative }}</td>
        <td class="figure">{{ row.net_benefit }}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

<p class="colophon">{{ doc.colophon }}</p>

</body>
</html>
